---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BleepAudio from '@/components/tools/redact/audio/BleepAudio.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bleep-audio" width="wide">
  <BleepAudio client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop an audio or video file: MP3, WAV, M4A, OGG, FLAC, MP4, MOV or WebM.</li>
      <li>Choose the spoken language and press Transcribe. The words appear as they are read.</li>
      <li>Names, numbers, places and emails are marked as black blocks. Press any word to bleep it or keep it.</li>
      <li>Add words to the always-bleep list, and choose a tone, silence or noise.</li>
      <li>Play the file to hear the result, then download WAV or MP3. For a video, download the video with the new sound.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What runs on your device</h2>
    <p>
      A speech recognition model (Whisper tiny, 33 MB) writes down each word with its start and end time. A second model (21
      MB) finds names, places and addresses in the transcript, and pattern rules find phone, card and other numbers. The
      runtime that runs both is 15 MB. All of it downloads once from this site and is kept in your browser, so the tool works
      offline after the first use.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bleep covers each marked word plus a short margin, because word times can be off by a tenth of a second or more.
      Make the margin larger if you hear the start or end of a word.
    </p>

    <h2>How to check that nothing is uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      Open your browser's developer tools, select the Network tab and transcribe a file. You see the model files load from
      this site the first time, and no request after that. You can also run the tool once, turn off your network and use it
      again.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The small model makes mistakes, more so with noise, music, accents and several people who talk at once. Read the transcript and listen to the result before you share it.</li>
      <li>English works best. Other languages are transcribed with less accuracy.</li>
      <li>A video keeps its picture as it was. Only the sound is written again.</li>
      <li>The whole file is decoded into memory. Recordings up to about an hour work on a laptop.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
